    options: ['@BindValue binds values to the Bundle', 'Hilt components cannot be tested with fake dependencies', '@UninstallModules is for uninstalling the app during tests', '@UninstallModules removes specific production modules from the test component. @BindValue binds a test field directly into the DI graph. Together they enable replacing production dependencies with fakes in instrumented tests.'],
    correctAnswer: 3,
    explanation: '@UninstallModules removes a module from Hilt\'s component for the test, allowing you to replace its bindings. @BindValue directly binds a field value into the Hilt component without creating a module. This is ideal for providing fakes/mocks. Use with @HiltAndroidTest and HiltAndroidRule for full integration testing.',
    codeSnippet: '@HiltAndroidTest\n@UninstallModules(NetworkModule::class)\n@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4::class)\nclass UserScreenTest {\n    @get:Rule(order = 0)\n    val hiltRule = HiltAndroidRule(this)\n\n    @get:Rule(order = 1)\n    val composeRule = createAndroidComposeRule<HiltTestActivity>()\n\n    @BindValue\n    val repository: UserRepository = FakeUserRepository()\n\n    @Before\n    fun setup() {\n        hiltRule.inject()\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun displaysUsers() {\n        (repository as FakeUserRepository)\n            .setUsers(listOf(User("Alice")))\n\n        composeRule.setContent {\n            UserScreen()\n        }\n        composeRule.onNodeWithText("Alice").assertIsDisplayed()\n    }\n}',
