    question: 'How do you implement lazy (on-demand) initialization with the App Startup library instead of automatic startup?',
    options: ['App Startup always initializes eagerly', 'Disable automatic initialization by removing the meta-data entry from the manifest and adding tools:node="remove". Then call AppInitializer.getInstance(context).initializeComponent(MyInitializer::class.java) when needed.', 'Set android:enabled="false" on the Initializer', 'Use a background thread for lazy initialization'],
    correctAnswer: 1,
    explanation: 'To defer initialization, remove the Initializer\'s meta-data entry from the manifest using tools:node="remove" on the entire provider, or on specific initializer entries. Then manually initialize when needed using AppInitializer.getInstance(context).initializeComponent(). Dependencies are still respected during manual initialization.',
    codeSnippet: '<!-- Disable automatic initialization -->\n<provider\n    android:name="androidx.startup.InitializationProvider"\n    android:authorities="${applicationId}.androidx-startup"\n    android:exported="false"\n    tools:node="merge">\n    <!-- Disable specific initializer -->\n    <meta-data\n        android:name="com.example.HeavyInitializer"\n        tools:node="remove" />\n</provider>\n\n// Initialize on demand later:\nclass FeatureScreen {\n    fun onFirstUse(context: Context) {\n        AppInitializer.getInstance(context)\n            .initializeComponent(\n                HeavyInitializer::class.java\n            )\n        // Dependencies are auto-initialized too\n    }\n}',
