    question: 'What is the effect of @NonRestartableComposable and when should it be used?',
    options: ['It makes the composable run only on first composition', '@NonRestartableComposable marks a composable that should not be a recomposition scope. It cannot independently recompose; changes propagate to the nearest restartable ancestor. Use it for tiny composables where the restart overhead exceeds the recomposition savings.', 'It disables state restoration for the composable', 'It prevents the composable from being called more than once'],
    correctAnswer: 1,
    explanation: 'Every restartable composable has an associated recomposition scope in the slot table. For very small composables (like Text wrappers), the overhead of maintaining this scope exceeds the benefit of targeted recomposition. @NonRestartableComposable removes this overhead, but the composable will always recompose when its parent does.',
    codeSnippet: '// The Compose team uses this for small composables:\n@NonRestartableComposable\n@Composable\nfun Spacer(modifier: Modifier) {\n    Layout({}, modifier) { _, constraints ->\n        layout(\n            constraints.minWidth,\n            constraints.minHeight\n        ) {}\n    }\n}\n\n// DO NOT use on composables that:\n// - Read state directly\n// - Are expensive to recompose\n// - Have many children\n\n// Good candidates:\n// - Simple wrappers with no state reads\n// - Layout-only composables with no logic',
