    question: 'How do CompositionLocal values propagate through the composition tree and what are the performance implications?',
    options: ['CompositionLocal values are global singletons', 'All CompositionLocals cause recomposition when read', 'CompositionLocal values propagate down the tree implicitly. staticCompositionLocalOf does not track reads (changes trigger full recomposition of subtree). compositionLocalOf tracks reads (changes only recompose readers). Use static for rarely-changing values.', 'CompositionLocal values must be explicitly passed to every child'],
    correctAnswer: 2,
    explanation: 'compositionLocalOf tracks which composables read the value, enabling targeted recomposition when the value changes. staticCompositionLocalOf does NOT track readers; when it changes, the entire subtree below the provider recomposes. Use static for values that rarely change (theme, locale) and dynamic for frequently-changing values.',
    codeSnippet: '// Static: no read tracking, efficient for stable values\nval LocalTheme = staticCompositionLocalOf { AppTheme.Default }\n// When changed: entire subtree recomposes\n\n// Dynamic: tracks readers, efficient for changing values\nval LocalUser = compositionLocalOf<User> { error("No user") }\n// When changed: only composables that read it recompose\n\n// Providing values:\nCompositionLocalProvider(\n    LocalTheme provides darkTheme,\n    LocalUser provides currentUser\n) {\n    // Subtree can access:\n    val theme = LocalTheme.current\n    val user = LocalUser.current\n}',
